THE MODIFICATION (2026-10-15)
-----------------------------
Added: a platform-level API endpoint that creates a tenant workspace
programmatically, plus the identity that owns such workspaces.

Reason: upstream Relaticle creates workspaces only through its Filament
registration UI (App\Filament\Pages\CreateWorkspace) or the `demo:reset`
artisan command. There is no REST route, console command or other programmatic
path — `php artisan route:list --path=api/v1` shows 34 routes and none of them
creates a workspace, and API tokens can only be minted from an in-app settings
page that itself requires a workspace and a signed-in user. An external
orchestrator therefore cannot provision a tenant end to end. That gap is the
entire reason for this fork.

Files added:
  app/Http/Middleware/EnsurePlatformKey.php
  app/Actions/Platform/ProvisionWorkspace.php
  app/Http/Controllers/Api/V1/WorkspaceProvisionController.php
  app/Console/Commands/EnsurePlatformServiceUserCommand.php
  NOTICE  (this file)

Files modified:
  routes/api.php        registers POST /api/v1/workspaces, outside the
                        workspace-scoped middleware group
  config/relaticle.php  adds a `platform` section: the X-Platform-Key shared
                        secret and the service-user identity
  Dockerfile            threads an overridable NPM_REGISTRY build ARG through
                        the frontend stage (the build host blackholes
                        registry.npmjs.org); no runtime behaviour changes

NO UPSTREAM LOGIC WAS ALTERED. The new endpoint calls upstream's own
App\Actions\Jetstream\CreateWorkspace — the same action the Filament page
resolves — so workspace creation retains a single code path and this fork stays
a thin addition that is cheap to rebase onto upstream.

The modification is confined to the `platform` branch. `main` tracks upstream so
that `git merge upstream/main` remains usable.

THE MODIFICATION (2026-10-16) — workspace teardown
--------------------------------------------------
Added: the delete counterpart to the provisioning endpoint, so the orchestrator's
cancel path can remove a tenant without a control channel into the container.

Files added:
  app/Actions/Platform/DeprovisionWorkspace.php
  app/Http/Controllers/Api/V1/WorkspaceDeprovisionController.php

Files modified:
  routes/api.php        registers DELETE /api/v1/workspaces/{slug}, in the same
                        platform-guarded group as the create route (a token
                        pinned to a workspace cannot exist for one being removed)

NO UPSTREAM LOGIC WAS ALTERED. DeprovisionWorkspace calls upstream's own
App\Actions\Jetstream\DeleteWorkspace (the DeletesTeams contract), so deletion
keeps a single code path — billing cancel, `purge()` cascade and media cleanup
all remain upstream's business. The route is idempotent: an unknown slug returns
`{"deleted": false}` rather than throwing.

THE MODIFICATION (2026-10-16) — exempt the platform service user from the cap
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Changed: App\Policies\WorkspacePolicy::create() now returns true for the
platform service user (config `relaticle.platform.service_user_email`) before
applying the per-user owned-workspace cap.

Reason: App\Actions\Platform\ProvisionWorkspace creates EVERY platform-provisioned
workspace owned by that one service user, and it delegates to upstream's own
CreateWorkspace, which authorises `create` through this policy. The upstream cap
(`relaticle.workspaces.max_owned_per_user`, default 10) is an anti-abuse limit for
HUMAN owners; applied to the single non-human provisioning identity it silently
capped the whole platform at 10 tenants, so the orchestrator's FATAL relaticle
provisioning step 403'd ("This action is unauthorized.") from tenant #11 on.

Files modified:
  app/Policies/WorkspacePolicy.php  `create()` exempts the service user; a private
                                    `isPlatformServiceUser()` matches the email
                                    against the `platform.service_user_email`
                                    config value. The cap still applies to every
                                    other user unchanged.

THIS IS THE FIRST FORK COMMIT THAT ALTERS AN UPSTREAM CODE PATH (the earlier
additions were new files only). The change is deliberately minimal and localised
to one method so it stays cheap to rebase; `main` is unaffected.


THE MODIFICATION (2026-10-21) — tenant-owned workspaces + a session broker
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Added: (a) a platform-guarded endpoint that mints a SINGLE-USE, short-lived
sign-in for a workspace's own owner, and (b) a public web route that redeems it
and establishes the panel session.

Reason: a brand that signed up on the platform console was still asked to log in
to the CRM — a second sign-up. The console brokers a module session from the
console login, but Relaticle's panel is session-based (Filament) and no Sanctum
bearer can stand in for a browser session. The hand-off is therefore a one-time
token the browser redeems, which is the only HTTP-only way in.

The same change gives a workspace an identity of its own. The first cut created
EVERY workspace under the shared platform service user, so no tenant had a
credential to broker AS, and all tenants shared one owner row. The owner is now
the tenant-scoped identity derived from the signup (`admin@<slug>.<base domain>`
— never a human's signup email, which is globally unique).

